## Summary of Key Steps

| | Dirichlet (part a) | Neumann (part b) |
|---|---|---|
| **Potential** | Double-layer $W=\iint\frac{z\nu}{r^3}\,d\xi d\eta$ | Single-layer $V=\iint\frac{\mu}{r}\,d\xi d\eta$ |
| **Harmonicity** | $\frac{z}{r^3}=-\partial_z(1/r)$, harmonic for $z>0$ | $1/r$ harmonic for $z>0$ |
| **Jump relation** | $W\|_{z=0^+}=2\pi\nu$ (p.v. $=0$ for plane) | $\partial_n V\|_{z=0^+}=2\pi\mu$ (p.v. $=0$ for plane) |
| **Density** | $\nu=\frac{f}{2\pi}$ | $\mu=\frac{f}{2\pi}$ |
| **Uniqueness** | Unique | Up to additive constant |

The principal-value integrals vanish in both cases because the plane $z=0$ is flat: the kernel $\cos\phi/r^2 = z/r^3$ and $\partial_z(1/r)=-z/r^3$ both vanish identically at $z=0$, leaving only the jump contribution $2\pi$ times the density.

